    "created_at": "2023-12-05T10:41:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-12-05T10:43:01Z",
    "body": "@domenic great question! That is a _much_ larger kettle of fish, but something we (the WECG) are working on.\r\n\r\nHistorically, the chrome.* APIs are only documented via the official Chrome extension documentation. This is the API that was copied by other user agents for their own compatible version of Web Extensions. It was never formally specified, and as a result there are a number of places with subtle differences. We are actively working on a WPT suite for the entirety of the Web Extensions platform to help find all of the places where there is a delta between implementations. \r\n\r\nIdeally, the WECG will graduate to a formal group that can publish it's own spec. Regardless of if or when that graduation occurs, the research is being done, the data collected, and the implementations becoming more aligned. Given the amount of time it will take for that to finish, it doesn't feel right to block on coalescing our documentation and API entry point on all of that work.\r\n\r\nSpecifically, \"what is the problem with one more nonstandard API, window.browser?\", @RByers may be able to speak more to it. But I think the idea is setting a relatively high bar for introducing new properties on `window`.",
