    "updated_at": "2023-12-08T22:31:09Z",
    "body": "I thought the idea was that we wanted to reserve the API name `window.browser` on the open web without currently specifying anything inside of it. I said from a blink API owners perspective I was happy to approve shipping the exposure of a name without any properties inside it simply as providing the benefit of reserving the namespace for use by extensions. This has some non-zero compat risk (sites might already be using \"window.browser\" for something that wouldn't shadow) so does need to go through our blink process, so needs a spec, idlharness WPT test, etc.\r\n\r\nNow I don't think it's true that CGs can't author specs. They aren't empowered to author standards track documents, but we author \"specs\" (and ship APIs based on them in chromium) in, for example, the WICG all the time. We expect those to graduate to standards-track venues (or be removed from chromium) with time. So from a blink process perspective I had assumed your CG would just produce a non-standards-track document that uses formal spec language to define this empty API. It could also be in someone's personal github if the CG really doesn't work. Or maybe compat spec is reasonable too? I don't really have an opinion. ",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
