    "created_at": "2023-12-12T01:08:43Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-12-12T01:08:43Z",
    "body": "The [compat specification](https://compat.spec.whatwg.org) is not necessary the right place either. \r\n\r\n> This standard describes a collection of web platform features that web browsers need to support for compatibility with the de facto web.\r\n\r\nUsually we have tried in the past to put in the compat spec, things that browsers **need to implement** to have a working Web page and where the technology has not been described in any specs. As much as possible it is also intended to be a temporary host. If the feature is absolutely necessary it should be described in the right WG/spec. \r\n\r\nSo the question for `window.browser` is:\r\n\r\nIs there a version of this which is absolutely necessary to be implemented by other browsers to **be compatible with the current Web**? \r\n\r\nOr is it just a matter to spec it in participation with other stakeholders in the appropriate group in the right document, until it gets buy-in from implementers.\r\n\r\nThe danger if the compat spec editors become too lenient on what goes in, it kind of becomes a troy horse for non-well defined technologies, which are not mandatory for interop. \r\n\r\n\r\n**Off-Topic**\r\n> Now I don't think it's true that CGs can't author specs. They aren't empowered to author standards track documents, but we author \"specs\"\r\n\r\nI miss the time when they were called [W3C Member Submissions](https://www.w3.org/submissions/).\r\n\r\n\r\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
