    "created_at": "2023-12-12T21:43:23Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-12-12T21:43:23Z",
    "body": "@rbyers \r\n\r\n> I thought the idea was that we wanted to reserve the API name window.browser on the open web without currently specifying anything inside of it\r\n\r\nYes, that is what we had discussed, and what the PR I have for this is doing\r\n\r\n> I don't think it's true that CGs can't author specs. They aren't empowered to author standards track documents...\r\n\r\nApologies, I was conflating the terms. Thanks for the correction!\r\n\r\n@karlcow \r\n\r\n>  The [compat specification](https://compat.spec.whatwg.org/) is not necessary the right place either.... [its] things that browsers need to implement to have a working Web page and where the technology has not been described in any specs\r\n\r\nThat was part of my question. I think that WebExtensions are generally expected to exist in any modern browser, and while the extension specification is not written yet, there is a mountain of documentation around the existing situation. Additionally, in order for [content scripts](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Mozilla/Add-ons/WebExtensions/Content_scripts) to work in an expected manner, there needs to be _some_ kind of global that is exposed from the web. That global is used to register event listeners and send messages to other extension surfaces. Some sites _do_ require this API to function (e.g. PWAs that only work with additional extensions, websites that use it for feature detection, etc). Given that chrome.* has been the way that WebExtensions have been written historically, and now browser.* is what all vendors are wanting to continue with, I thought that it made sense to exist in the Compat spec. \r\n\r\n> Is there a version of this which is absolutely necessary to be implemented by other browsers to be compatible with the current Web?\r\nOr is it just a matter to spec it in participation with other stakeholders in the appropriate group in the right document, until it gets buy-in from implementers.\r\n\r\n\r\nI think the biggest concern is a major website/library clobbering the `globalThis.browser` [namespace ala mootools and String.contains](https://github.com/mootools/mootools-core/issues/2402), or a new browser engine using window.browser for an unreleased purpose, making WebExtensions harder to work cross browser.\r\n\r\n\r\n> Not much... \r\n\r\nFWIW, Firefox and Safari already use browser.* APIs, so it isn't likely they would have bugs as a result of not having it. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
