    "created_at": "2023-12-13T02:01:29Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-12-13T02:03:48Z",
    "body": "@karlcow Hey there! Web Extension bugs for WebKit can be found in the [WebKit Extensions](https://bugs.webkit.org/buglist.cgi?bug_status=UNCONFIRMED&bug_status=NEW&bug_status=ASSIGNED&bug_status=REOPENED&bug_status=RESOLVED&bug_status=VERIFIED&bug_status=CLOSED&component=WebKit%20Extensions&list_id=9915714&order=bug_id%20DESC&product=WebKit&query_format=advanced) component.\r\n\r\n### Safari & WebKit Context\r\n\r\nSafari has implemented `window.browser` as an alias for `window.chrome` for Web Extensions on macOS since 2020, and on iOS since 2021. The implementation is also moving to [WebKit](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/Extensions/Cocoa/WebExtensionControllerProxyCocoa.mm#L80) as of late 2022.\r\n\r\nApple is absolutely committed to `window.browser` being the canonical home for Web Extension APIs in Safari and WebKit.\r\n\r\n### Compatibility\r\n\r\nWe have not seen any compatibility issues with `window.browser` being exposed.\r\n\r\nWe have had compatibility issues when it comes to exposing `window.chrome` to webpages for [external messaging](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/Extensions/Cocoa/WebExtensionControllerProxyCocoa.mm#L80), since exposing `window.chrome` caused too many sites to assume Safari `===` Chrome. So in the main JavaScript world of webpages, we can only expose `window.browser` because of that.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
