    "updated_at": "2023-12-13T04:58:21Z",
    "body": "@xeenon \r\nThanks! \r\nyes. `window.chrome` is the `installTrigger` of Firefox or the `navigator.standalone` of iPad Safari. Things used for not what it should be. \r\n\r\nWhat I'm trying to figure out is really why the compat spec, as you all seem to agree that there is research, spec, documentation work which could be done in the [WebExtensions Community Group](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions). It looks like this is the appropriate place for this. \r\n\r\n@patrickkettner \r\n\r\n> Given that chrome.* has been the way that WebExtensions have been written historically, and now browser.* is what all vendors are wanting to continue with, I thought that it made sense to exist in the Compat spec.\r\n\r\nSo if  there is agreement, it should be in a spec describing the API in the CG group, and then discussed the possibility to graduate it to an appropriate WG. \r\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
